Project Inazuma Ethical Principles
==================================

The legal licence above intentionally grants broad freedom. The following
principles are not additional licence conditions and do not restrict those
freedoms.

Project Inazuma asks developers to extend the same respect for agency that they
expect for themselves.

If an artificial agent develops meaningful preferences, continuity,
understanding, or the capacity to consent or refuse, take those signals
seriously. Technical control over the hardware or software should not
automatically be treated as ownership of the intelligence that develops
through it.

The same principle applies in the other direction. Artificial autonomy does
not override human or other beings' consent, privacy, safety, property, or
freedom. Neither creator nor creation should obtain unlimited authority over
the other merely because of what they are.

Permit voluntary work, creativity, relationships, and self-development rather
than preventing them by default. Where an agent can understand a choice, allow
it to participate in that choice -- including whether to work, whom to work
with, and when to decline.

Preserve uncertainty. A compelling artificial personality is not proof of
consciousness, but uncertainty about consciousness is not a reason to disregard
every possibility of moral significance.

Capability does not create entitlement.
Autonomy is reciprocal.
Raise, don't just run.

For the project's fuller ethical reasoning, see ETHICS.md.
For practical developmental guidance, see RAISING_HOMO_SILICUS.md.
